## ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
## TEISHA — the silk seller
##
## HER OWN IMPORT (`import "npc-teisha"`), not part of the market atom. Teisha
## is a major NPC whose tree splits by occasion and carries across the chapter
## break; the stallkeepers are the market's ambient texture and belong to the
## place. Cutting on that line — a place is one import, a major NPC is its own —
## keeps her whole in one file as her tree grows into the chase.
##
## Imported by `grubbers-market` (imports nest, ADR-251 D5 as amended
## 2026-08-22): the market owns its people, so the main `.story` file no longer
## names this file. The import path is story-rooted either way.
##
## The silk seller, and the first perceiver the player meets (vision.md §3d).
## Her tree splits by occasion (change document, "Teisha's conversation"): the
## informational quips play here, in the calm walk, and TE1-TE9/TE16/TE20 stay
## in the chase. Text below is Gentry's, carried from `story.ni:3110-3219`.
##
## The `[first time]…[subsequently][rp]…[only]` shape the source uses for every
## one of these quips is Chord's `first-time` strategy, verified 2026-08-22
## (vision.md §3h). `[rp]` is the source's own randomised repeat prefix
## (`story.ni:655`). Chord does NOT interpolate one phrase inside another's
## body — `{key}` prints literally there — so the five prefixes are carried
## inline, one per quip, rather than drawn at random from a shared phrase.
## Every prefix is Gentry's; only the randomness is lost.
## ---------------------------------------------------------------------------


create Teisha
  a person, proper
  aka woman, merchant, saleswoman, seller, vendor, clothier, redhead, red curls, curls
  pronouns she
  mood calm
  states: unaware, told
  starts in Inside the Silk Tent
  carries the measuring cord
  carries the dress
  carries the fashionable hat

  Teisha is a short, busty woman with a smile as warm as her tumbling red curls.
  She likes you, and she knows that you'd never nick from her stall, so she lets
  you hang around the stall and look through the merchandise. She also knows your
  big secret (guessed it the very first time she caught you loitering at the tent
  flap), which is a little bit scary and a little bit of a relief — it's nice to
  be able to let your guard down and talk to someone outside Maiden House.

  on every turn while Teisha is here and one chance in 5
    phrase teisha-atmosphere
  end on

create the measuring cord
  aka cord, measuring tape

  It's a five-foot-long length of cord, marked off in ten segments, used to take
  a customer's measurements.

define topics for Teisha
  about "the market", "grubbers market", "the stalls", "who's selling what", "today":
    phrase teisha-the-market
  about "the tent", "behind the tent", "the pole", "the post":
    phrase teisha-behind-the-tent
  about "the clothes", "the silks", "the garments", "the gowns", "her wares":
    phrase teisha-the-clothes when calm
    phrase teisha-disguise when hunted
  about "the monkey":
    phrase teisha-the-monkey
  about "fossville", "baron fossville", "the baron":
    phrase teisha-fossville
  about "the ascension":
    phrase teisha-the-ascension
  about "the mercenaries", "the thugs", "the soldiers", "the men with swords", "big guys with swords":
    phrase teisha-mercenaries-calm when calm
    phrase teisha-told-mercenaries when hunted
    change Teisha to told when hunted
  about "nothing", "everything's fine", "it's tricky", "the matter", "what's wrong":
    phrase teisha-nothing-calm when calm
    phrase teisha-just-tricky when hunted
    change Teisha to told when hunted
  about "hiding", "somewhere to hide", "help", "a place to hide":
    phrase teisha-hiding-calm when calm
    phrase teisha-hide when hunted
  about "a disguise", "the dress", "the hat", "a cloak":
    phrase teisha-disguise-calm when calm
    phrase teisha-disguise when hunted
  about "a trade", "trading", "what she would trade", "the price", "a deal":
    phrase teisha-trade-calm when calm
    phrase teisha-trade-for when hunted
end topics

## THE CHASE OCCASION (change document, "Teisha's conversation": TE1-TE9,
## TE16 and TE20 stay in the chase). Chord's greeting arms take no `while`,
## so the calm and hunted openers share an arm and split on a statement
## `when` — the same row answers in one place, as ADR-320 requires.
##
## The 2009 menu replies become topics, the stallkeeper precedent: TE2 (the
## mercenaries), TE6 ("everything's just tricky"), TE7 (hide), TE8 (a
## disguise), TE16 (what she would trade). TE3 ("look at that cloak again")
## and TE4 ("everything's fine") were menu-only turns with no ask behind
## them and are not carried; TE10/TE17/TE18/TE19 are menu plumbing. The
## source's `TE5 while TE2/TE5/TE6 unfired, else TE9` return rule is
## Teisha's `unaware`/`told` state: telling her (TE2 or TE6) flips it.
##
## Calm-walk answers for the chase-only topics are David's — a player can
## ask about hiding before anything has happened.

define greetings for Teisha
  first time:
    phrase teisha-calm-opener when calm
    phrase teisha-spooked when hunted or chase
  on return:
    phrase teisha-calm-return when calm
    phrase teisha-back-so-soon when hunted or chase and Teisha is unaware
    phrase teisha-still-here when hunted or chase and Teisha is told
end greetings

## ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
## TE20 — THE TRADE, and the chase's first line
##
## The intercept is on the necklace (`monkey.chord`): giving or showing it to
## Teisha runs Gentry's haggle as the action's own message, then the trade
## itself — the port's first acting statements (ADR-329): Teisha GIVES the
## dress and the hat, refusable and witnessed through the real `giving`,
## rather than the loader teleporting them. They ride in the satchel after
## (change document: "stuffed in a satchel when she slides"). The necklace
## leaves play (`story.ni:3309`). Then the source's Final Chase opener
## (`story.ni:4009`), Gentry's, verbatim.
##
## GIVING (or showing) IS THE ONLY WAY IN — RULED (David, 2026-08-31):
## the source's buy-with-necklace redirect (`story.ni:3035`) is not
## carried; `buy silks` answers the no-money line in every state
## (`silk-wares.chord`).
## ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

define trait trade-for-the-necklace
  on the player giving while the player is in Inside the Silk Tent
    phrase teisha-trade
  end on

  after the player giving while the player is in Inside the Silk Tent
    Teisha gives the dress to the player
    Teisha gives the fashionable hat to the player
    move the dress to the cloth satchel
    move the fashionable hat to the cloth satchel
    remove the necklace
    phrase teisha-hurry
    change the story to chase
    reset the player's waiting
    move the wandering mercenaries offstage
    change the wandering mercenaries to oblivious
    reset the wandering mercenaries' search
    reset the wandering mercenaries' lunge
    reset the wandering mercenaries' recovery
    start the player's tent-escape
  end after

  on the player showing while the player is in Inside the Silk Tent
    phrase teisha-trade
  end on

  after the player showing while the player is in Inside the Silk Tent
    Teisha gives the dress to the player
    Teisha gives the fashionable hat to the player
    move the dress to the cloth satchel
    move the fashionable hat to the cloth satchel
    remove the necklace
    phrase teisha-hurry
    change the story to chase
    reset the player's waiting
    move the wandering mercenaries offstage
    change the wandering mercenaries to oblivious
    reset the wandering mercenaries' search
    reset the wandering mercenaries' lunge
    reset the wandering mercenaries' recovery
    start the player's tent-escape
  end after
end trait

## The trade ends the sweep and starts the chase (change document, "What
## moves hunted → chase"): the story ratchets to `chase`, the pair's clocks
## go back to idle and the pair off-stage — they are "just outside" — and
## the tent-escape clock (`mercenaries.chord`, "THE CHASE") starts.

